{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '123,154p;286,312p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '69,87p;141,158p;473,510p;536,553p;603,612p;796,845p;879,890p;939,943p'\"","aggregated_output":"    69\t## Invariants\n    70\t\n    71\t- Stream elements are data. DaoStream assigns them no intrinsic meaning.\n    72\t- Reading is non-destructive. A reader advances its own cursor, not the stream.\n    73\t- Multiple cursors may observe the same retained value independently.\n    74\t- Interpreters own cursors and decide when to call `next`.\n    75\t- A runtime may drive an interpreter, but DaoStream does not require a runtime.\n    76\t- Closing a stream changes future availability but does not itself erase\n    77\t  retained history.\n    78\t- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap\n    79\t  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.\n    80\t- All expected operational outcomes are data, not exceptions or callbacks.\n    81\t- No operation waits. Every operation returns what is true at the moment it is\n    82\t  called; an answer not yet knowable arrives later as data.\n    83\t- No hidden global state. DaoStream keeps no registry of streams, handles, or\n    84\t  observers, and no operation consults ambient state.\n    85\t- A logical stream has an identity that is plain data, the same through every\n    86\t  handle on it, stable across attachment lifecycles and across serialization.\n    87\t  Projecting that identity never fails.\n   141\t**Non-blocking polling.** The operation returns immediately with whatever is\n   142\ttrue now, including the answer \"nothing yet, ask again.\" Nothing parks and\n   143\tnothing is inverted: the caller keeps control and decides when to ask again.\n   144\tThis is POSIX `O_NONBLOCK` without `select`, and it is DaoStream's model.\n   145\t\n   146\t`next` is total and non-blocking (see Reading): every state of the stream\n   147\thas a defined data answer, and `:dao.stream/blocked` is `EAGAIN` — this\n   148\tposition holds nothing yet. `append!`'s `:dao.stream/full` is the same\n   149\tanswer for writes. No operation in this contract parks, and none takes a\n   150\tcallback; the Result Convention's \"never exceptions and never callbacks\" is\n   151\tthis model stated as a rule about results.\n   152\t\n   153\tThe rule generalizes past `next`: **no operation waits.** An operation that\n   154\tcannot yet know its answer returns the outcome true at the moment it is\n   155\tcalled, and the eventual answer arrives the way every other fact does — as\n   156\tdata, observed later, through an ordinary operation or on a stream. A\n   157\tDaoStream operation is never a request for something to happen and be\n   158\treported back. It is a question about now.\n   473\t## Cursors\n   474\t\n   475\tA cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.\n   476\t\n   477\t- Cursors are minted by the stream: `cursor` on a handle takes an **anchor**\n   478\t  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract\n   479\t  defines two anchors — `:dao.stream/oldest`, the earliest retained\n   480\t  position, and `:dao.stream/newest`, positioned after the newest appended\n   481\t  value so the first `next` observes the next value to arrive. A transport\n   482\t  whose medium is naturally addressable may accept additional\n   483\t  transport-owned anchors (a byte offset, a timestamp). An anchor that is\n   484\t  malformed or not honored by this transport yields\n   485\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,\n   486\t  `cursor` yields `:dao.stream/closed`; a handle on the logical stream\n   487\t  itself keeps minting cursors after close, so retained history remains\n   488\t  readable. Minting may have to consult the medium — locating the earliest\n   489\t  retained position on a durable log is itself a read — so where a\n   490\t  transport's reads can fail at runtime, minting can too, and `cursor`\n   491\t  yields `:dao.stream/transport-error` for it; a transport whose medium\n   492\t  cannot fail this way excludes the outcome like any other (see Surfaces).\n   493\t  `cursor`'s outcome set is therefore `:dao.stream/ok` (with\n   494\t  `:dao.stream/cursor`), `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`,\n   495\t  `:dao.stream/closed`, and `:dao.stream/transport-error`.\n   496\t- A composition that intends to observe events caused by an operation mints\n   497\t  its `:dao.stream/newest` cursor **before** invoking that operation. Minting\n   498\t  after `attach!`, endpoint bind, handoff installation, or any other operation\n   499\t  that can deposit would intentionally skip events deposited in between.\n   500\t- **A freshly minted `:dao.stream/oldest` is a position, not a claim about\n   501\t  completeness.** The anchor names the earliest *retained* position, which on\n   502\t  an evicting transport advances as values are lost. A `gap` is reported to a\n   503\t  cursor that spans an eviction; a cursor minted after one is a valid cursor\n   504\t  onto the surviving suffix and reports nothing, because nothing is wrong with\n   505\t  the cursor. Retained history is not complete history, and no anchor can make\n   506\t  it so. A consumer that requires complete history gets it from the transport's\n   507\t  declared retention (see *Retention and Gaps*), never from an anchor.\n   508\t- A composition on a transport that **can evict**, intending to detect whether\n   509\t  history was lost, mints an **origin cursor** — `:dao.stream/oldest` before\n   510\t  the first append — and keeps it, exactly as a composition that intends to\n   536\t## Reading\n   537\t\n   538\t`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.\n   539\tIt is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data\n   540\tanswer.\n   541\t\n   542\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |\n   543\t|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n   544\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |\n   545\t| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   546\t| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |\n   547\t| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\n   548\t| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |\n   549\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   550\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the read; nothing was observed.               | —                                                                                       |\n   551\t\n   552\tReading never mutates the stream. Two readers with cursors at the same\n   553\tposition observe the same retained value; neither can starve or affect the\n   603\t`:dao.stream/closed` and `:dao.stream/end` are handle-relative: they speak of\n   604\twhat *this handle* is on. (Not every outcome is: `gap` is retention-relative\n   605\tand `cursor-mismatch` is logical-stream-relative.) After an attachment\n   606\tcloses, the handle's `append!` answers `closed`; `descriptor` still answers\n   607\t`ok` with reachability and identity — both outlive any attachment; a reader surface, where the\n   608\thandle has one, answers `end` from `next` once observation through this\n   609\thandle is exhausted, and `closed` from `cursor` — no new cursors are\n   610\tminted through a closed attachment. Cursors bind to the logical stream,\n   611\tnot to a handle, so a kept cursor resumes through a newly attached handle.\n   612\t\n   796\tStatus: **proposed, binding nothing.** Every other section of this document is\n   797\tcontract; this one is not. Each entry names an assumption the contract makes\n   798\twithout stating it, the use that would break it, and a proposed resolution.\n   799\tAn entry leaves this section in one of two ways: accepted, its text moves into\n   800\tthe section it amends and the entry is deleted; or rejected, the entry is\n   801\treplaced by one sentence in *Explicitly Absent* recording why. Drafted\n   802\t2026-09-19 from a review of this document against `dao.stream`, its three\n   803\ttransports (`ringbuffer`, `memory-log`, `ws`), and their consumers.\n   804\t\n   805\tOD-1 to OD-4 are ordered by cost of delay. Each is a paragraph today, while\n   806\tthree transports and a handful of consumers exist, and a migration once a\n   807\tdurable or replicated transport ships. OD-5 was added afterwards and is\n   808\tnumbered by arrival; it concerns the layers above this contract rather than the\n   809\tcontract's own operations.\n   810\t\n   811\t### OD-1. An unrecognized outcome has no defined meaning\n   812\t\n   813\t**The assumption.** The Result Convention makes each operation's outcome set\n   814\texhaustive and makes result *maps* open. The two rules are asymmetric: a future\n   815\textension may add a key, never an outcome. The contract assumes the seven\n   816\toutcome sets are complete for every transport that will ever exist.\n   817\t\n   818\t**Evidence.** Consumers are already total over the closed sets and treat\n   819\tanything else as a fault: `dao.stream.observe/step` classifies an unrecognized\n   820\tread or effect outcome as `transport-error` and reports `:defect` or `:failed`;\n   821\t`yin.vm.engine/handle-put` and `handle-next` throw. *Envelopes* declines to\n   822\treserve an outcome for gating and says gating \"arrives as an addition\", but\n   823\tunder the exhaustive rule a new outcome is not an addition.\n   824\t\n   825\t**What breaks.** Any condition the present outcomes cannot express honestly:\n   826\tattachment or append refused for lack of authority; a descriptor that is valid\n   827\tbut served elsewhere now; a transport that is throttling rather than full; an\n   828\tappend whose effect is unknown (OD-2). Each must either be squeezed into an\n   829\texisting outcome that misdescribes it (`not-found` for unauthorized, `full` for\n   830\tthrottled) or break every consumer.\n   831\t\n   832\t**Proposed resolution.** Keep each outcome set exhaustive *for this version of\n   833\tthe contract* and add one rule to the Result Convention that makes later\n   834\toutcomes safe to introduce:\n   835\t\n   836\t> A consumer that receives an outcome it does not recognize treats it as: not\n   837\t> `ok`; nothing observed and nothing appended; and not retryable, unless the\n   838\t> result map carries `:dao.stream/retry? true`. It does not throw, because an\n   839\t> unrecognized outcome is a newer contract, not a defect in this host's\n   840\t> assembly.\n   841\t\n   842\tWith that rule a newer transport degrades to \"refused, do not retry\" on an\n   843\tolder consumer instead of to an exception, and `:dao.stream/retry?` lets it\n   844\tsay when waiting is the right response. The existing `full` and `blocked` keep\n   845\ttheir meanings and need not carry the key.\n   879\t1. Reword `transport-error` on `append!`: \"The transport failed to perform the\n   880\t   append. Whether the value was appended is unknown unless the transport\n   881\t   declares that its failures are clean.\" The three existing transports declare\n   882\t   clean failure, so nothing changes for them.\n   883\t2. State the rule the RPC layer already follows: **deduplication and\n   884\t   correlation are the payload's.** A writer that must retry safely puts an\n   885\t   identity in the value; a reader that must not act twice deduplicates on it.\n   886\t   The stream never promises exactly-once.\n   887\t3. Name where later answers land: a transport whose acceptance is not its\n   888\t   final answer (durability reached, far-end refusal) declares the channel on\n   889\t   which that answer is deposited, as *Close* already requires for a failing\n   890\t   flush.\n   939\t2. **Settle serialization.** Replace \"TBD\" with: \"A cursor is plain data and\n   940\t   survives the host serialization codec structurally unchanged, under the same\n   941\t   rule as the envelopes. A cursor that outlives the stream it names yields\n   942\t   `not-found` at `attach!` or `cursor-mismatch` at `next`, never a defect.\"\n   943\t   Both existing transports already satisfy this.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '123,154p;286,312p'\"","aggregated_output":"   123\t### 3.6 Anchors across a hop\n   124\t\n   125\t`cursor` has no `blocked` outcome, yet a proxy cannot consult the source synchronously. The design does not invent a proxy-namespaced cursor (that would put a cursor in circulation that the source's own handle rejects, violating \"any handle on the same logical stream accepts it\"). Instead the server **piggybacks fresh `:oldest` and `:newest` cursors on `:serve/opened` and on every response**, and the proxy's `cursor` answers with the most recently observed one.\n   126\t\n   127\tThis is honest under the contract's own definitions. A stale `:newest` is *earlier* than a fresh one, so a composition that mints `:newest` before invoking an operation still observes everything the operation causes; it may additionally observe a few values appended between the last exchange and the mint, which is re-seeing, never skipping. A stale `:oldest` may point at a position since evicted, and the contract already says a fresh `:oldest` is a position rather than a completeness claim: the reader receives `gap` with the recovery cursor. Before the first `:serve/opened` the proxy holds no anchors and `cursor` answers `transport-error` with `:dao.stream/retry? true` (OD-1's proposed key); the composition observes `:serve/opened` on the proxy's event medium and mints then.\n   128\t\n   129\t### 3.7 Held reads (optional, v1 decision needed)\n   130\t\n   131\tAcross a network a `blocked` answer costs a round trip. A `next` request may carry `:serve/hold true`; a server that honors it records the demand in session state and, on each of its own driver steps, re-polls the source, answering when the outcome is no longer `blocked` or when its hold budget (a constructor policy in the driver's clock domain) elapses, in which case it answers the `blocked` it has. No operation waits: the session holds state, the step returns. A proxy must tolerate a server that ignores the flag. Whether v1 includes this is an owner decision (Q2).\n   132\t\n   133\t## 4. The proxy handle: declared nature\n   134\t\n   135\tA proxy is a handle minted by `attach!` on a serve descriptor. It declares exactly the surface the `:serve/opened` frame reports, which is the source's declared surface; until then it declares the surface the descriptor's `:serve/surface` hint names, and a mismatch at open is a session failure deposited as data. `descriptor` returns the serve descriptor and the identity. The proxy is composed as three explicit pieces: the handle (an object over an explicit state atom, as `WsHandle` is), an **event medium** the composition wires and the channel adapter deposits into, and `proxy-step`, which the composition's driver calls with `now`.\n   136\t\n   137\tThe handle's `next` is a read of the proxy's **window**, a bounded host-local cache keyed by cursor value (structural equality; every cursor a reader holds was minted by the source, through this proxy or another). A miss records a **demand** in proxy state and answers `blocked`. `proxy-step` issues demands as requests, installs responses into the window, opens sessions, retries by the composition's policy, and evicts the window oldest-first. The window is a cache, not retention: an evicted window entry is re-fetched, and only the source ever answers `gap`.\n   138\t\n   139\t| Op        | Produces                                                                 | Excluded, and why |\n   140\t|-----------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------|\n   141\t| `cursor`  | `ok` (last observed anchor, §3.6), `invalid-anchor` (relayed), `closed`, `transport-error` (`:establishing`, retryable; or channel failure) | — |\n   142\t| `next`    | every source outcome, relayed verbatim; `blocked` also for *not here yet*; `end` for positions beyond the window after the session ended; `cursor-mismatch` locally by identity; `invalid-cursor` for a non-map; `transport-error` for `:oversize` or channel failure | — |\n   143\t| `append!` | `ok` (accepted into the session's outbound path, carrying `:dao.stream.serve/id`), `full` (one append already outstanding, or channel outbound full; transient), `invalid-value` (outside the channel's domain), `closed`, `transport-error` | — |\n   144\t| `close!`  | `ok`                                                                    | — |\n   145\t| `descriptor` | `ok`                                                                 | — |\n   146\t\n   147\tThe proxy's writer surface is on the **session's ordered outbound path**, as ws's is on the connection's; the source's answer to an append arrives on the proxy's event medium as `{:serve/event :serve/appended :dao.stream.serve/id n :serve/outcome <map>}`. This is the contract's Writing rule and the ws precedent, and it has a cost for the UCF (Q1).\n   148\t\n   149\tSession loss is detachment, not ending: the proxy deposits `:serve/detached`, `append!` answers `closed`, and `next` answers what the window holds and then `end`, exactly as the contract specifies for a closed attachment. Cursors bind to the logical stream, so the reader's kept cursor survives; a fresh `attach!` with the same descriptor resumes at it. Re-establishing a lost session under the same proxy handle, answering `blocked` meanwhile, is a **proxy policy the composition supplies as data** (`:reconnect {:attempts n :after-ms t}`); the default is none, so nothing retries invisibly.\n   150\t\n   151\tTwo subtleties the contract forces:\n   152\t\n   153\t- `end` from the source is relayed. `end` from session loss is local. Both mean \"what this handle is on is closed\" and both are correct; a program that reads `end` as *the stream ended* will misread a channel loss unless its composition reconnects. This is the same limit ws documents under *Ending a served stream*, and the same answer: the serve `:serve/close` frame from the server carries `:serve/reason :ended` when the source's owner closed it, so the proxy can deposit `:serve/ended` versus `:serve/detached` and a composition can tell which it saw.\n   154\t- A proxy is not a retaining medium and has no `gap` of its own. It never produces a `gap` the source did not.\n   286\t## 13. Completion criteria\n   287\t\n   288\t1. **Loopback conformance.** Serve a memory log and a ring buffer through an in-process channel pair (two ring buffers as the two directions). The proxy passes the contract's reader and writer tests, and every cursor the proxy hands out is structurally equal to one the source minted.\n   289\t2. **Gap fidelity.** Evict at the source under a kept cursor; the proxy relays `gap` with the source's recovery cursor, with no replay and no skip.\n   290\t3. **Anchor semantics.** A `:newest` minted through the proxy never skips a value appended after the mint.\n   291\t4. **UDP under loss, reorder and duplication** (a lossy socket seam): no duplicate append at the source, no lost read, no proxy-fabricated gap, and `:serve/append-unknown` deposited when the session dies with an append in flight.\n   292\t5. **Outbound serving.** A peer that only dials out serves a stream to a peer that dialed the same relay; the relay's state never holds a session.\n   293\t6. **Punch and fallback.** Two UDP peers behind a NAT simulator open a direct channel through a rendezvous; with a symmetric-NAT simulator they fall to the relay candidate, reported as data.\n   294\t7. **P2P symmetry.** Two peers each serve one stream to the other over one channel.\n   295\t8. **UCF §7.11 facade acceptance**, as written there: park with a string-backed stream at a kept cursor, migrate, resume, force eviction, observe `gap`; a non-portable source refuses at lift; an unreachable endpoint refuses at lower.\n   296\t9. **Portability.** Items 1–4 and 7 run on clj, cljs node and cljd; item 5 also on cljs browser through the ws relay path.\n   297\t\n   298\t## 14. Deferred\n   299\t\n   300\tListed in §12. Additionally: a copy-serving mode remains useful (broadcast to many readers with local retention) and is what `dao.stream.serving` already is; it should be renamed or re-described so that \"serving\" no longer suggests attachment to the original stream (C2).\n   301\t\n   302\t---\n   303\t\n   304\t# Open questions for the owner\n   305\t\n   306\t- **Q1. Append through a proxy.** As designed, `append!` on a proxy answers `ok` for acceptance into the session's outbound path and the source's outcome arrives later on the event medium, as ws does and the contract's Writing section allows. The UCF's `:put` rule (\"retries its retained value and resumes on `ok`\") would then resume a program on an append the source may still refuse. Alternative: the proxy answers `full` until the source's outcome arrives and then reports that outcome, holding the value meanwhile, which gives the writer the source's truth from `append!` itself at the cost of a proxy-level convention (one held value, writer retries the same value). Recommendation: keep the contract-consistent design and amend UCF §7.4.3 so that a `:put` on a served stream resumes when the source's outcome is observed, not on outbound `ok`.\n   307\t- **Q2. Held reads in v1.** They cut network chatter for `blocked`-heavy readers such as a parked VM polling a response cell, at the cost of per-session server state and a hold budget. Recommendation: include, since the UCF's dominant pattern is exactly a kept response cursor polled until a value lands.\n   308\t- **Q3. Peer identity.** Opaque composition-assigned strings now, self-certifying kickoff-hash identities later, or self-certifying from the start. Recommendation: opaque now; the relay routes by string equality either way, and signature verification is an additive rule on `:serve/register`.\n   309\t- **Q4. Relay and rendezvous gating.** v1 accepts any `:serve/register` and `:serve/dial`. Recommendation: accept for the dev-only repo, record postage as the intended answer.\n   310\t- **Q5. Accept OD-1, OD-2 and OD-3 (a)+(2) now.** This design is not implementable without OD-3 and is dishonest without OD-2; OD-1 is needed only for the `:establishing` answer. Recommendation: accept all three as a precondition of the implementation phase.\n   311\t- **Q6. Lifetime of a served entry after migration.** Lease-governed (recommended, via `dao.lease`), or composition-retired with `not-found` as the honest late answer.\n   312\t- **Q7. Naming.** `dao.stream.serve` for this layer and a rename of today's `dao.stream.serving` to `dao.stream.serving.copy` or similar, so the two models are not confused.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1790338900000-architect-dao-stream-serve-q1-q7-mob-r5.glm-5.3.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1790338900000-architect-dao-stream-serve-q1-q7-mob-r5.glm-5.3.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-25 12:27:27 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 19:27:27 +07\n     3\t\n     4\t# dao.stream.serve — round 5, Q5(i) sequenced close (GLM-5.3)\n     5\t\n     6\tI read codex's round-4 reply verbatim and re-verified every line it cites before answering: the anchor definitions (`dao.stream.md:477-481`), the Reading table (`:542-550`), the no-waiting rule (`:153-158`), `end`'s handle-relativity (`:603-609`), the design's anchor piggyback and window-miss behavior (findings.md:125-127, 137), the completion criteria (findings.md:286-296), and the throwing consumer path (`engine.cljc:245-254`). All accurate.\n     7\t\n     8\t## A. Do codex's two amendments resolve my objection? — YES; Q5 is settled. I accept with three specific edits.\n     9\t\n    10\tFirst, what happened in the exchange: codex r4 concedes both points my round-3 position turned on — no new outcomes, no synchronous source consultation (\"These changes use existing outcomes and return immediately, so they respect no waiting and require no unaccepted OD\"; \"GLM's arguments establish that a fresh remote answer cannot be demanded synchronously and that current VM consumers throw on a new outcome\"). What it holds instead is that my *mechanism* was too weak: a subordinate §4 declaration plus one marginal sentence cannot change what the contract's own definition sites say — the two affected definitions must be amended where they live. On the text, codex is right, and by my own citation: the serve doc is subordinate (\"Where the two disagree, the contract wins\", findings.md:6), and the contract's own amendment protocol moves accepted text *into the section it amends* (`dao.stream.md:799-801`), not beside it. Amending the definitions is my declaration position, executed properly. I checked both drafted blocks against the contract and the design:\n    11\t\n    12\t- **The blocked amendment** makes `blocked` handle-relative (\"no observation is available *through this handle* at this cursor now\"). That fits the contract's existing relativity structure — it already classifies `closed`/`end` as handle-relative and `gap` as retention-relative (`dao.stream.md:603-605`) — and changes nothing for direct transports, for which \"no observation available through this handle\" and \"nothing at this position yet\" coincide. The deferred-fetch clause matches the design exactly (findings.md:137, 142).\n    13\t- **The anchors amendment** (\"source-minted anchor from its last completed source observation\"; \"`:newest` may precede the source's current tail but must never follow it\") states the provable invariant: an anchor observed at time t₀ is ≤ the source's tail at t₀, hence ≤ the tail at any later mint, so the no-follow clause holds even under UDP response reordering — and it preserves the cursor-namespace rule, since piggybacked anchors *are* source-minted (findings.md:64, 73, 103). The `:oldest` clause matches the design's §3.6 gap-with-recovery-cursor behavior (findings.md:127) and criterion 2 (findings.md:288).\n    14\t\n    15\tThree edits, none changing substance:\n    16\t\n    17\t1. **\"A later successful source observation supplies the source's outcome\" — change \"successful\" to \"completed.\"** As drafted, \"successful\" could be read as *ok-only*, which would wrongly exclude exactly the outcomes the design relays verbatim: the source's own `gap`, `end`, and `blocked` (findings.md:142: \"every source outcome, relayed verbatim\"). \"Completed\" (matching \"last completed source observation\" in the Cursors block, which already has it right) says what is meant: the fetch finished; whatever the source answered is what the reader gets.\n    18\t2. **Keep `blocked`'s retry disposition.** The current row says \"Nothing at this position yet; **retry later**\" (`dao.stream.md:545`); the replacement drops it. Consumers are owed the EAGAIN contract (the IO Model's own gloss, `dao.stream.md:146-148`). Restore \"; the reader may retry later\" to the first sentence.\n    19\t3. **One declared-nature term across both amendments.** Block 1 says \"deferred remote reads,\" block 2 \"deferred remote anchors\" — two names for one nature on one handle. Surfaces declarations are per-handle (`dao.stream.md:446-453`); unify to a single term (e.g. \"deferred remote observation\") used in both blocks, so a handle declares one nature governing both `next` and `cursor`.\n    20\t\n    21\tI also accept codex's framing that this is a serve-design conformance gate independent of the OD bundle — decoupling is cleaner than my \"rides with the OD acceptances,\" and the amendments indeed presuppose no OD.\n    22\t\n    23\t## B. Is codex correct that `end` cannot be \"only ever relayed\"? — YES; my round-3 phrasing was wrong.\n    24\t\n    25\tThe contract makes `end` explicitly handle-relative: \"`:dao.stream/closed` and `:dao.stream/end` are handle-relative… a reader surface, where the handle has one, answers `end` from `next` once observation through this handle is exhausted\" (`dao.stream.md:603-609`). The design itself relies on this — after session loss, \"`next` answers what the window holds and then `end`… `end` from the source is relayed. `end` from session loss is local. Both… are correct\" (findings.md:149, 153). My round-3 sentence \"with `gap`/`end` only ever relayed\" was therefore wrong for `end`, and I correct it: **`gap` is only ever relayed, never synthesized** (findings.md:154; retention-relative, so a proxy-local `gap` would be fabrication); **`end` is either relayed (source ended) or local and contract-sanctioned (attachment exhausted)**. Codex's reading of `dao.stream.md:603` is accurate.\n    26\t\n    27\t## C. Remaining disagreement\n    28\t\n    29\tNone. The one open item of round 3 is closed on codex's amended mechanism with the three edits above; the OD dispositions (Q5(ii)) were already agreed.\n    30\t\n    31\tAll seven questions now carry a position both models endorse: Q1 as-designed + UCF §7.4.3 amendment (with `append-unknown` named); Q2 defer held reads; Q3 opaque composition-assigned ids in trusted v1 + defined duplicate-registration, public path deferred to channel-auth work; Q4 composition-supplied relay admission with bounded state, open default only for trusted deployments, postage as future policy; Q5 OD-2 + OD-3(a)+(2) as drafted, OD-1 with the corrected write fallback accepted for the implementation phase, and the proxy's cached-read semantics legalized by the two definition amendments + §4 declaration; Q6 lease-governed with composition retirement as the pre-grant/v1 stopgap and renewals counted only on observed source `ok`; Q7 `dao.stream.serve` plus the copy-serving rename and ws prose fix.\n    32\t\n    33\tCONSENSUS: Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, Q5, Q6, Q7; OPEN: none\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
